<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simone de Beauvoir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Simone Lucie Ernestine Marie Bertrand de Beauvoir, Castor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simone de Beauvoir was a French existentialist philosopher, writer, social theorist and feminist activist with a significant influence on feminist existentialism and feminist theory. In 1929 she was placed second in the agrégation in philosophy, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a>, at 21 the youngest person to pass it. Her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-second-sex">The Second Sex</a> (1949), a detailed analysis of women's oppression, became a foundational tract of contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a>. She also wrote the novels She Came to Stay (1943) and The Mandarins (1954), and memoirs beginning with Mémoires d'une jeune fille rangée (1958); she received the 1954 Prix Goncourt and the 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Prize.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir.jpg" alt="Simone de Beauvoir" width="330" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moshe Milner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simone_De_Beauvoir_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Beauvoir was born on 9 January 1908 in Montparnasse, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Her father, Georges Bertrand de Beauvoir, was initially a lawyer but aspired to be an actor, and her mother, Françoise, was a devout Catholic and a banker's daughter. The promised dowry was lost when Françoise's father was jailed. Her parents were often absent, and she was raised chiefly by a nanny, Louise. She had a younger sister, Hélène, born on 6 June 1910. Intellectually precocious and encouraged by her father, she wrote that the contrast between her father's individualism and pagan standards and her mother's rigid conventional morality made her life an endless disputation, the main reason she became an intellectual.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After her studies Beauvoir saw teaching as a way to earn her own living. She did her practice teaching at the same secondary school as Maurice Merleau-Ponty and Claude Lévi-Strauss, and from 1929 to 1943 she taught at lycées in Marseille, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> until she could live on her writing. She then became a public intellectual. Her first novel, She Came to Stay, appeared in 1943, followed by The Blood of Others (1945), which tells a love story between two young students in the French Resistance during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a> and Maurice Merleau-Ponty she co-founded Les Temps Modernes, a monthly political journal that carried communists, Catholics, Gaullists and socialists, whose first issue appeared on 1 October 1945. Sartre and Merleau-Ponty later quarrelled, and Beauvoir sided with Sartre. She remained an editor until her death and in later years held the editorial meetings in her flat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Her first philosophical essay, Pyrrhus et Cinéas (1944), was followed by The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947), in which she argues that the freedom of the self requires the freedom of others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-second-sex">The Second Sex</a> (1949) turned the existentialist principle that existence precedes essence into a feminist one: One is not born, but rather becomes, woman. It articulated the distinction between biological sex and the social construction of gender, and showed how sexual difference is used to trap women in the role of the Other. The Mandarins (1954), a roman à clef about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Sartre</a>'s and Beauvoir's circle after the war, won the Prix Goncourt.</p><p>Beauvoir at first denied being a feminist but later admitted it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-second-sex">The Second Sex</a> opened the way for second-wave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a>, and Kate Millett, Shulamith Firestone, Juliet Mitchell, Ann Oakley and Germaine Greer read it in translation and acknowledged their debt to her. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betty-friedan">Betty Friedan</a> said that reading it in the early 1950s led to her own analysis of women's existence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simone de Beauvoir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in 6th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in 14th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.85,2.33&amp;year=1958" aria-label="6th arrondissement of Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Simone de Beauvoir"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5131 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5131 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.85,2.33&amp;year=1958">What was here in 1958? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
